{
  "image_name": "0eb09265d886a81357e67d0c3d0c5b1e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts an immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment under low versus high glucose conditions and shows how metabolism shapes immune cell behavior. In the upper part, a low-glucose environment favors regulatory T cells and suppressive myeloid cells. Tumor cells and MDSCs express enzymes such as CD39, CD73, and IDO, converting ATP to adenosine and tryptophan to kynurenine. These metabolites signal through A2A/A2B receptors and the Kyn–Ahr pathway, promoting FOXP3-positive regulatory T cells and fatty-acid oxidation while dampening effector T-cell glycolysis and cytotoxicity. Additional inhibitory interactions, including PD-1/PD-L1 and CTLA4, reinforce this suppressive state.\n\nIn the lower part, a high-glucose environment appears to shift the balance toward antitumor immunity. Effector T cells maintain glycolysis and cytotoxic function, while regulatory T cells rely less on fatty-acid oxidation and show more glycolytic behavior. Adenosine signaling is still depicted, but the overall suppressive network seems reduced. The schematic therefore presents glucose availability as a key determinant of whether the tumor niche supports immune suppression or effective T-cell attack, linking nutrient competition with checkpoint signaling and metabolite-mediated regulation.",
